WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.690
Film Courage: Should a writer aim for a certain
amount of rejections per year and if they’re

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.599
not getting those rejections then [maybe]
that means they’re probably not getting

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:10.099
out there enough?

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:16.309
Is that an interesting way to look at it or
maybe I am being too negative here?

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.199
That is entirely possible.

00:00:18.199 --> 00:00:22.480
Kathie Fong Yoneda: You know, I don’t know
if there is such a thing as a certain number

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.810
of rejections.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:31.090
Whether you are setting the bar for something
for failure or not.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:40.240
Instead I would turn it around and say Okay,
I think once a week set a goal for doing at

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.899
least one piece of marketing or two pieces
of marketing.

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:48.210
I think that’s a better way of looking at
it than saying “Okay I’ve had ten, I’ve

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:52.950
reached the threshold of ten rejections this
month so I’m not going to do anything now.”

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.000
That to me is more negative.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.980
So I would say “Okay let’s do this in
a week.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:07.080
Why don’t I look up five different places
that I can send out letters or that I can

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:19.470
send emails to or why don’t I look up ten
different resources for screenwriting competitions

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:20.860
or something.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.890
Doing something like that I think is much
more productive because you never know when

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:26.890
one of those are going to come.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:34.329
But to just say “Well, okay if I did this
many rejections then that means I quit.”

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:38.000
Because who knows if you didn’t do that
next one.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.500
Film Courage: Define pieces of marketing?

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.299
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Marketing just means making
the effort to get out there whether it’s

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:52.840
posting something…there are places that
there are places that you can post your story

00:01:52.840 --> 00:02:00.469
ideas and your synopses and then if someone
is interested they will then contact you and

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:04.130
then you can let them look at your script
and you can get your script read.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:14.160
So you can post your projects, to me it’s
not just posting a project it’s also like

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:22.330
making plans to enter a competition, making
plans to go to a conference or something.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.890
Those are positive things that are moving
in the direction and you have to invest in

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.890
yourself.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:31.380
I understand that people go “Yeah but it
costs money to enter a competition.”

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.900
Or “Yeah it costs money to go to a conference.”

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:45.370
Well if you’re really serious about it you
have to make the investment in yourself somehow

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:50.430
and so maybe you have to be a little bit more
frugal about things.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:55.370
Okay maybe I can only spend ten dollars or
something or twenty dollars or whatever it

00:02:55.370 --> 00:03:01.050
is a week that I’m going to sock that away
for it but whatever it is to you make those

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.510
plans.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:11.730
I have clients who did it for years before
something happened for them but now things

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.620
are working for them or they really feel like
they can’t stop and they’re really doing

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.940
some good work.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.400
And it takes a lot of time.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.070
You can’t just expect it to happen overnight
because it doesn’t and certainly that survey

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:31.120
I took of all those professional writers proves
that it doesn’t work overnight.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.520
It took that one writer who did twelve scripts
before he got an agent.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.920
I think he said he wrote those twelve scripts
over fifteen years?

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.270
Film Courage: Oh, wow!

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.130
And it sounds like you were very surprised
to hear that number as well?

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.140
Kathie Fong Yoneda: I was.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.950
This is a guy who was a top writer.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:04:00.670
You would think that something must have happened
and that right away it would continue and

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:01.670
continue.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:08.250
And also like the one gal who on her first
script got somebody to read it and buy it

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:14.370
but it took her five years and four more scripts
before she got somebody to option her second

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.870
one and it never got made unfortunately they
optioned it at a studio and then a new set

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:24.630
of people came in and they didn’t want to
continue with that particular script but you

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.810
never know I mean that’s how this business
is.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.000
Film Courage: You know, I am hearing this
a lot “a regime change.”

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.250
And how should someone stay current because
it is difficult when a new group of people

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:41.260
who don’t know you and maybe they had sort
of a relationship with you [previously with

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:42.260
the old group] come in.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.810
How do you ingratiate yourself to a new regime?

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.920
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Well, it’s hard to personally
do that.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:54.790
But to be aware of it what you need to do
is and especially if you have a writer’s

00:04:54.790 --> 00:05:03.480
group if you guys together get a subscription
to The [Hollywood] Reporter or Variety or

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:11.120
whatever one you choose and you guys share
that subscription and in fact there are people’s

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.310
who have writers groups where people take
turns doing it so that once a month they rattle

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:23.910
off “Okay, here’s what I read and here’s
the latest news that I’ve gotten.”

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.040
Then the next person next month another person
does it.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.730
So that everybody is well-read but reports
everything back to everyone and I think that’s

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.090
a nice way of being able to do it.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:36.990
It’s networking but it’s also getting
out really good solid information.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.700
Hey, so-and-so is no longer at the head of
this.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.670
This production company hired a new story
editor.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:50.370
It’s information that is valuable to the
whole group and that’s how you keep current.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:55.710
Naturally if you have an agent or a manager
they should already know these things but

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:01.290
don’t count and this is another (I think)
mistake that a lot of writers make.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:05.340
They feel that if they have an agent or a
manager they don’t need to do anything.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:12.000
And one of my friends, one of my best friends
actually has won an Oscar.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.210
And she tells me that a lot her, a lot of
the things that happen, it’s not because

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.960
she has an agent or a manager.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:25.850
It’s because she made the effort to go out
and talk to people and find out what was going

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:33.920
on and even though she lives out of town (she
doesn’t live in Los Angeles) but she keeps

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.630
up with email and phone calls and she is in
touch with a lot of the producers and production

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:45.221
people that she used to do business with when
she was living down here and she says it’s

00:06:45.221 --> 00:06:51.120
been the most valuable lesson that she’s
learned to keep those contacts and send Christmas

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.120
cards.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.110
Or give them a call every once in a while.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.350
And you’d be surprised.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:00.880
One of the things is that she happened to
write a movie for somebody who was a star

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.160
of a television show.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:12.560
And I’m not going to mention the name of
it or anything but that actress her star really

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:21.870
rose and what happened is my friend happened
to see her at a Writer’s Guild event and

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:27.070
went up to her and talked to her and the woman
looked at her and she goes “Oh my gosh.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:31.220
You’re the one who wrote the script for
that movie of the week that helped my career

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.640
shoot up!”

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:39.790
And it was like she remembered her name because
of that I think.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:44.690
And so she says “If you get a chance, if
you’re writing something and you think I

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:47.630
might be good for it, please let me take a
look at it.”

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:52.830
And sure enough a year later my friend sent
it to her and they took a look at it.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.480
They don’t know if anything has come of
it yet.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.070
But I do know that it’s something that is
on their mind so it’s on their radar and

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.070
they might be doing it.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:08.060
There’s other material that she’s doing
(other commitments she has) so you never know.

